LEGENDARY, AS SIX SKINS -- the measured output of
dev/legendary/BUILD_LEGENDARY_PAINT.luau, one run per framing.

Both runs calibrate the same six shapes to the same 45% coverage, so the two
tables differ only in the body size the cells were built at. The feature columns
are reported at BOTH framings from either run, which is the whole point of
having two of them.

FOUR RULES NOW, AND THE LAST TWO WERE EACH ADDED AFTER A FAILURE THE EXISTING
ONES COULD NOT SEE.

RULE 3's HUE TERM. `rosette` -- lilac (185,140,235) with deep violet (60,25,110)
rings -- rendered as ONE COLOUR WITH TEXTURE and measured lum 0.462 (2.9x the
ladder's worst) and rgb 0.827 (1.08x). It is 4 DEGREES apart in hue: the same
violet at two brightnesses, which is exactly what a light does to one colour.
The terminator rule in colour space. contrast() came from BUILD_SKINS where the
accent is always white; white has no hue, so the number never varied there.

RULE 4, THE EYE ZONE. `sweeps` (then `ocelli`) shipped five navy rings with two of them at 15
degrees elevation, 52 degrees either side of the nose. Coverage 44.8%, feature
37.9 px, J 0.18, lit 1.05, all three contrast numbers clear -- and it rendered
as a FOUR-EYED SLIME. A symmetric pair of round marks at the eyes' own height
does not decorate a face, it joins it. This is a PLACEMENT constraint, and
neither the coverage gate nor the contrast gates can see it: those ask what a
design is made of, this asks where it was put.

The zone is solved out of FACE_PARTS rather than declared -- eye_dark is one
mesh holding both eyes, so its bounding box is solved back into two spheres
(half-separation 0.335 body radii, radius 0.117, at 0.812 forward):

    |azimuth| <= 30.1 deg,  elevation -15.1 .. 19.9 deg

    sweeps (old)  el 15.0  reaches  8.1 deg from the midline   IN BAND  -- fails
    koi           el 17.4  reaches 30.5 deg from the midline   in band  -- passes
    cow           el 25.0  reaches  6.1 deg from the midline   out band -- passes

KOI CLEARS BY 0.4 DEGREES, which is not a margin. The gate is calibrated on one
failure and two passes with one of the passes on the line: a flag is a reason to
look at the render, never a proof. The robust fix is not to tune against it --
the design that ships has NO mirrored pair anywhere, so it cannot be near the
threshold at all. Asymmetry breaks the eye reading outright and costs nothing.

THE NAME. This design was `ocelli` -- eyespots -- and at these radii it is not
that: each ring has a 44 deg outer radius with its centre 52 to 62 deg off the
nose, so a viewer sees the near half of each annulus and never a whole ring with
its hole. What renders is four broad curved sweeps. Renamed for the picture
rather than the construction.

